Prerequisites
VMware Workstation (Pro or Player) installed
An x86-compatible Android ISO (e.g., from android-x86.org or Bliss OS)
Minimum 4 GB RAM on host; 8 GB+ recommended
At least 20 GB free disk space
Virtualization enabled in BIOS/UEFI (Intel VT-x or AMD-V) 
Step-by-step instructions
Open VMware Workstation and click Create a New Virtual Machine
Choose Typical (recommended) setup
Select I will install the operating system later
Choose Other > Other 64-bit as the OS type
Name the VM (e.g., "Android-Victim") and set a storage location
Allocate at least 4 GB RAM and 2 CPU cores
Set virtual disk size to 20 GB or more, stored as a single file
Complete the wizard, then go to VM Settings > Processors
Enable Virtualize Intel VT-x/EPT or AMD-V/RVI
Optionally enable 3D graphics acceleration under Display settings
Mount the Android ISO: Go to CD/DVD (SATA) > Use ISO image file and browse to your downloaded Android-x86 ISO
Power on the VM
At the boot menu, select Install Android-x86 to harddisk
Choose Create/Modify partitions, then use cfdisk to:
Create a New Primary partition
Make it Bootable
Write the changes and Quit
Select the new partition and choose ext4 as the filesystem → Confirm with Yes
When prompted, install the GRUB bootloader → Yes
Choose No for read-write system (keeps system intact across reboots)
After installation, go to VM > Removable Devices > CD/DVD > Disconnect to eject the ISO
Reboot the VM
At the GRUB menu, press e to edit boot parameters
Find the line starting with kernel and replace quiet with:
nomodeset xforcevesa

Press Enter, then b to boot
Android should now start with GUI
To make the fix permanent:
After boot, open terminal (Alt+F1)
Run: mount -o remount,rw /
Edit GRUB config: vi /grub/menu.lst
Add nomodeset xforcevesa to the kernel line
Save with :wq, then reboot
Configure network: Use Ethernet with DHCP enabled
Optionally create a snapshot in VMware for easy reset (ideal for victim machine use)
